00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.980
Kubernetes, as you all know,
is a container orchestration

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.800
system that automates
deployment, scaling,

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.310
and management of
containerized applications.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:37.510
It groups all the containers
belonging to an application

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:41.560
into logical units for easy
management and discovery.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.980
It builds upon many years
of Google's experience

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.125
in running production workloads.

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:51.710
It is able to be run on
major public clouds like GCP,

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:53.050
AWS, Azure.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.240
And with the latest
technologies,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.530
it is also able to run
on the on-prem clusters.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:03.370
It handles all the
common cluster routines,

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:07.600
like scheduling of the life
cycle, scaling, et cetera,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:08.770
on behalf of users.

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.570
Kubernetes is an
API-centric system.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.230
What this means
is that everything

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.980
communicates through the APIs.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.080
The API server is the center
of universe in Kubernetes.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.790
It powers all the APIs.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:31.960
It serves the REST endpoints
supporting all the common CRUD

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:36.310
operations like create,
get, update, delete.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:40.000
It also stores the API
resources, the objects,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.860
in etcd.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:44.710
Amongst all the
components in Kubernetes,

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:47.620
such as the [INAUDIBLE]
like kubectl,

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.560
the control print
component like scheduler,

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.500
and the node agent
like kubelet, have

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.040
to talk through the API server.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:01.160
They have to send a request
to the server for processing.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.090
Now, let us have a view
at the basic building

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.320
blocks of Kubernetes.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.260
In Kubernetes, we have
this concept named pod.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:16.149
A pod is basically a group
of tightly coupled containers

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:20.290
belonging to an application
and the shared volumes.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:24.280
We group all the containers
of an application together,

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.670
and also the necessary
underlying volumes

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.230
within this pod.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.440
And all the containers
within the pods

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.410
share the same life
cycle and phase.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:38.070
So if you have one pod--

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.500
if a pod is down, all
the containers within it

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.420
is also down.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.530
And the containers also share
the same IP and pod space.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.640
They see each other
as localhosts.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.520
There are three
types of containers--

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.690
the init containers,
main containers,

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:57.580
and sidecar containers.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.090
Init containers perform
initialization routines

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:05.260
like creating data directory
or downloading the source codes

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.450
before the main
container is started.

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:11.140
The init container has
to be completed before we

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.330
can start the main container.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:17.350
Main container is where the
real application is running.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:21.950
For example, as an end user, if
I deploy a redis application,

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:25.750
the application will be run
between the main container.

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:27.910
And lastly, the
sidecar container

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:32.440
is a container that
provides additional routines

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.340
and functions to assist
the main container.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.085
Some examples are monitoring
and logging sidecar.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:43.240
One of the most important
features in Kubernetes

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:49.030
is that it allows the end
users to specify the desired

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:51.160
states, the target
states, the states

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:54.190
that they want of the
cluster to Kubernetes

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:56.470
using declarative APIs.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:06:01.660
This means that the users
only need to tell Kubernetes

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:06.070
how many replicas do they want,
and Kubernetes will basically

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:07.030
do everything.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:10.490
Perform the reconciliation
underneath automatically.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.300
It's different
from imperative API

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.610
in which the users
have to provide

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.600
step-by-step instructions.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:23.320
In Kubernetes, all the API
resources, all the objects,

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:28.220
have an API that can be broken
down into three components.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.760
The first component
is metadata in which

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:35.170
you can put the name, namespace,
the labels, et cetera.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:37.570
And the second
component is a spec.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:42.340
Spec API fields is where the
users provide the desired

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:46.930
states that they want for
this particular API resource.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:50.910
And lastly, the status field
is where the Kubernetes

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:54.580
will update the current
status, the current states

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:57.880
at a current point of
time of the API resource.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.940
For example, what is
the number of parts

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:04.990
that are already ready at this
moment, and how many of them

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:06.330
is still not ready yet?

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.480
Now, let us go to
another important concept

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:13.860
in Kubernetes--

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.230
the controller.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:18.660
A controller is the long
lived controlled route

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:21.840
that performs the
automatic reconciliation

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:24.090
on behalf of users.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:27.940
The reconciliation is
made up of three steps.

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:32.910
First, the controller observe
the existing current states

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:34.530
of the cluster.

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:39.640
It then compares those states
with the desired states,

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:43.440
the declared states
provided by the users.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.740
It deduces the
differences, and then it

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:49.290
acts upon the differences
by bringing the cluster

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.600
to the desired states.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.840
For an example, let's
say we have a cluster.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.570
And that cluster runs two
application instances.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.140
However, the user
specified that there

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.990
is a need to have three
number of replicas

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:06.100
running at any given time.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:09.000
So what happens here
is that the controller

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.000
will create another
replica to measure

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.450
that the total number
of replicas is always 3.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:19.620
Similar thing happens, let's say
initially we have 4 replicas.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:23.490
The controller will then delete
one of the extra replicas.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:30.230
And now let us go over some
primitives in Kubernetes.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:33.650
Primitives, or the built-in
controllers of Kubernetes,

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:37.760
is widely used for
running the applications.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:41.210
I believe most of you
have already used them.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:42.750
I will go through some of them.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:44.740
The first one is ReplicaSet.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:47.450
ReplicaSet is a
controller that maintains

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:51.140
a set of pods, of
identical pods,

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.870
at any given point of time.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:57.110
It guarantees availability
of a certain number of pods.

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:00.410
If a pod is down, it
will always restart it.

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:02.730
The next control is deployment.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.790
Deployment is a little
bit complicated,

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:07.760
but it is a higher
level controller

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:11.780
that provides declarative
updates for the ReplicaSets

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:12.740
and pods.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:17.080
What happens is when a user
creates the primary source,

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:19.280
a corresponding
ReplicaSet or resource

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:23.060
will also be created by
the deployment controller.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.130
And then the
ReplicaSet controller

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:29.510
that we mentioned just
now will create the pod.

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:34.250
Hence, the deployment controller
will manage all the pods

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:38.260
indirectly through the
ReplicaSet controller.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.160
And lastly, it is the
StatefulSet controller.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.330
A StatefulSet controller
is used specifically

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:47.120
for managing the
stateful applications.

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:50.230
When we mention about the
stateful applications,

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:56.180
we refer to those applications
that depends on the data states

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:59.360
to run the operations
accurately.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:09:59.990
Yeah.

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:01.880
All the parts
within a StatefulSet

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:04.250
maintain a sticky identity.

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.480
That has a persistent
identifier that

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.270
will be given the same
throughout any re-scheduling

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:12.350
of the pod.

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:14.360
For the first part
of a StatefulSet,

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:16.580
we have the index number zero.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:19.700
And for the second one,
we have one, and so on.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.640
For the next session,
Sunil will guide us

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:26.740
through extending Kubernetes.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.480
SUNIL ARORA: Let's
look at how Kubernetes

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.380
have evolved with respect
to running applications.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:35.110
So initially, it started
with stateless apps,

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:36.550
which are very simple apps.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:39.310
And if you look at the
operations involved

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:44.140
in managing the lifecycle of
stateless apps is on Day 0,

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:45.940
you would just orchestrate them.

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:49.090
And Day 2 would pretty much
require either scaling them

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:53.080
up or down, or would require
updating, which is a roll-out.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:56.650
So those operations
are supported

00:10:56.650 --> 00:10:59.590
by the existing primitives
like ReplicaSet and Deployment.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:01.690
You can just use those,
and they are good enough

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.940
to run stateless workloads.

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:09.520
Now, moving on to
simple stateful apps,

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:11.410
these are apps which
are not distributed.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.340
They are kind of
very simple to run.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.010
So for that, StatefulSet
and CSI gives you

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.100
a good enough primitive
to run them successfully

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:20.590
for Day 0 and Day N operations.

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:24.610
Now, let's look at a
complex app, which is--

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:26.770
think of a distributed
application like a data

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:30.770
store or, let's say, a messaging
application like Kafka.

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:34.820
So on Day 0, it
involves orchestration,

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:37.890
which is setting up
the initial topology.

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:42.160
Day 2, typically that's where
the real trouble starts,

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:44.740
which is let's say you
want to take backup

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:46.000
or you want to restore.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:47.800
Depending on the
application, application

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:51.590
may have its own storage
semantics for that.

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:54.190
Now, update might involve--

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:56.650
these applications have their
own clustering mechanisms,

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:59.920
so update might require a lot of
coordination between the nodes.

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:03.280
Similarly, when it
comes to scaling them,

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:05.690
which is you want to
adjust the topology,

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:09.790
so that would also require
coordination between the nodes.

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:11.600
It gets really complicated.

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:13.360
And so it goes on.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:15.140
You want to reconfigure
the same thing.

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:20.390
So if you have such
an application--

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:23.030
now, this is beyond
Kubernetes knowledge

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:28.560
to provide primitives to operate
these independent applications.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.410
So what would have
been really nice

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.110
if you could model
all these operations

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:37.040
of these complex applications
as declarative APIs.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.950
And you can use the same
tooling like kubectl

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:41.420
to operate on them.

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:44.300
And so for example
here, it would be nice

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:46.320
if I were to launch
a DB instance.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:50.210
I could just set, here is a
spec from my DB instance, which

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:54.290
is readReplicas is 2, storage
is I want this sort of storage,

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:55.850
I want backup to
be done weekly, I

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:59.060
want backupStorage to
be using slow disk,

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:00.350
and things like those.

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:02.600
And I can perform all those--

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:04.340
I can manage all
those operations

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:07.580
using the kubectl API--

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:08.330
sorry, tooling.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:16.250
So Kubernetes doesn't
give you those primitives.

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:20.370
But it provides these
extension mechanisms

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:23.850
which you can use to build
such an experience on top

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.020
of Kubernetes.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:27.408
So let's look at when
Kubernetes was started,

00:13:27.408 --> 00:13:28.950
it was started with
the goal of being

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.140
just a container
orchestration system,

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:34.580
but over the years, what I've
been seeing last two years,

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:37.770
team and community has
put in so much effort

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:39.570
in making the system extensible.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:42.930
And now it has evolved
into a platform.

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:48.150
So it provides constructs
which you can build on top of.

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:50.310
So when it comes to
running application,

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:53.230
it is just not running
application within Kubernetes.

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:55.980
It is a lot of stuff which
is around application which

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.180
people have to worry about.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:58.637
It is policy enforcement.

00:13:58.637 --> 00:13:59.970
It's more continuous deployment.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:03.600
So I think with evolving
it as a platform,

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:07.650
I think there is a lot of pieces
that community has contributed

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:11.370
if you look at that is making
the whole platform really

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.030
powerful, as well.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:15.810
So there are two dimensions
to extending Kubernetes.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:18.060
One is infrastructure
extensibility,

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:20.850
which is extending it to
different hardware providers,

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:22.470
different storage providers.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:24.570
The second is API
extensibility, which

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:27.730
is what we'll be
focusing on today.

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:29.550
So in the next few
slides, I'll be

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:33.900
covering the two main important
API extensibility mechanisms.

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:36.060
And then we'll go
back and reconnect

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:38.490
with the slide we talked
about earlier, how to manage

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:40.118
the complex application.

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:46.020
So the first primitive
is custom resource.

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:49.770
And you must have heard of
this term, CRD, everywhere.

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:54.870
So custom resource is another
API provided by Kubernetes

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:59.400
which allows you to introduce
your own API in the cluster.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:02.280
And to give you an example--

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:04.490
so custom resource definition,
how do you do that?

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:08.910
So custom resource definition is
another declarative yaml file.

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:12.720
You specify the sort of
what I would call schema

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:14.730
for your custom resource.

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:18.880
Think of it as, let's say,
if you have a SQL database,

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:19.710
you would define--

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:21.585
when you create a table,
you define a schema,

00:15:21.585 --> 00:15:24.660
and then you have
rows in the table.

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:27.470
In the similar way,
CRD is the definition,

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:29.550
is the schema for your API.

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:32.340
And custom resource
instance are--

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:37.140
think of them as the actual
instances for that resource.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.630
So let's say once you
have this yaml file which

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:43.100
defines the schema
for custom resource

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:46.927
where you can specify the API
group diversion and the kind,

00:15:46.927 --> 00:15:48.510
and then there are
additional features

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:52.110
that CRD supports, for
example, OpenAPI validation.

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:55.650
You can specify what
sort of validation

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:58.620
needs to be performed, then
printer column support,

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:02.230
which I'll cover later
when I do a live demo.

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:03.720
So the way to
think about this is

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.300
once you submit
this yaml file, then

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:10.290
Kubernetes API server gives you
this hosted endpoint service.

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:13.500
It will automatically
create a REST endpoint

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:16.020
for that API resource.

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:19.650
And now this API is available
to all the existing tooling

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:21.700
and clients in order to consume.

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:24.420
So the experience is
very similar to what you

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:25.850
will have for built-in APIs.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:34.070
So the second primitive
extension point

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:39.850
that Kubernetes provides
is the admission webhooks.

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:43.470
So if you look at the lifecycle
of a request in the Kubernetes

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:47.280
API server, let's say an
incoming resource like Pod,

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.650
when it hits the
API server, it will

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:52.050
go through the series of
admission control plugins.

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:54.660
There are some built-in
admission control plugins,

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:59.010
but there are two new ones which
got introduced in last year

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:02.010
or so is validating webhook
and mutating webhook.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:03.310
So let's look at one by one.

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:06.750
So validating webhook is
where Kubernetes API server

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:10.109
for each incoming resource
can make a request

00:17:10.109 --> 00:17:12.780
to an external HTTP web service.

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:15.780
And that service can say
whether to accept or reject

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:17.369
this incoming resource.

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:19.890
Now, this can be
used to implement

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:21.652
some very powerful mechanisms.

00:17:21.652 --> 00:17:23.819
Like, let's say you want
to enforce certain policies

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:27.780
in your system, which
a simple example would

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:30.480
be you want to allow only
certain images to be running

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:31.110
in Kubernetes.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:33.780
So this would be
one way to do it.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:35.710
Let's look at another
extension point,

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:37.923
which is mutating webhook.

00:17:37.923 --> 00:17:40.131
So let's say you have an
incoming resource, like Pod,

00:17:40.131 --> 00:17:48.240
and Kubernetes API sort of
will call another HTTP API

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.740
service which can mutate the
spec of the incoming resource.

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:55.240
So in this case, let's
say you can modify the Pod

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:58.657
and then it will be
persisted in that [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:58.657 --> 00:17:59.490
after going through.

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:01.800
So the modification--
this enables

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:04.210
some of the really
powerful use cases.

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:06.630
One would be, let's say there
are things that you want

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:08.940
to do across your applications.

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:12.330
One would be let's say you
want to inject a sidecar

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:14.950
container for log collection.

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:17.550
So you can do such a thing
using mutating webhook.

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:20.210
Or let's say for
some other-- let's

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:24.930
say Istio is one where they use
sidecar injection for the NY

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:26.410
proxy and things like those.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:30.690
So another thing where
mutating webhook is used which

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:33.100
is for defaulting.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:35.250
So if you have an
API and user has not

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:36.930
specified certain
fields and you want

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:38.640
to put in some
sensible defaults,

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:43.470
that would be another use
case for mutating webhook.

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:47.780
So we covered CRD and we
covered admission webhook.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:50.440
And now let's go back
and reconnect the dots

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:54.420
with how do you manage a complex
application which is very--

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:59.160
which has-- managing
Day 2 operations

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:00.880
are very unique to
that application.

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:03.160
So you take these
three building blocks.

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:08.370
First is we use the CRD to
introduce the declarative APIs.

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:11.820
Then you take all that
operational knowledge

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:14.460
of your application which
might be sitting either

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:17.010
in playbooks or some
bunch of bash scripts,

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:20.780
or maybe sometimes
in wiki pages.

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:23.280
So you can take that
operational knowledge,

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:27.270
put that in the
controllers, and with that

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:30.960
you will have a working API
to manage your application.

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:33.870
And now optionally, you
can use admission webhooks.

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:39.190
Now, when it comes to
orchestrating and managing

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:41.500
all the workflows
related to application,

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:45.140
you can leverage all the
Kubernetes primitives.

00:19:45.140 --> 00:19:48.550
For service discovery,
you can use the services.

00:19:48.550 --> 00:19:52.840
You can use Pod,
StatefulSet, or ReplicaSet

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:57.700
for orchestrating what I call
as infrastructure programming.

00:19:57.700 --> 00:19:59.440
You can use secrets
and conflict map

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:02.260
for conflicts and
credential management.

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:04.535
So you don't have to reinvent
the wheel over there.

00:20:04.535 --> 00:20:06.160
And if you look at
some of the building

00:20:06.160 --> 00:20:08.870
controllers are
also done like that.

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:11.290
ReplicaSet is built
on top of pods

00:20:11.290 --> 00:20:13.910
and deployment is built
on top of ReplicaSet.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:18.310
And so there is a hierarchy
of resources there.

00:20:18.310 --> 00:20:21.550
And once you have that,
you use the existing tools

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:23.450
like kubectl to manage
the application.

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:27.700
So to the end users, it's
built-in API, pretty much,

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:28.450
for them.

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:30.370
And that's the whole idea.

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:31.810
And you get all
the other features

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:34.000
like authorization,
authentication, and auditing

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:36.790
that comes with
the API for free.

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:44.300
So now we know that, OK, these
are the primitives available

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:49.610
and you can build your custom
controllers, application

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:51.690
controllers, to manage
your application,

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:53.880
now let's look at
how we can do that.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.926
So in this section, we'll be
talking about Kubebuilder.

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:09.130
So Kubebuilder is an open source
project under Kubernetes org.

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:11.800
It's actually a subproject
of special interest group API

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:13.640
machinery.

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:15.950
So the project was
started about a year back

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:18.890
and we looked at
the state of the art

00:21:18.890 --> 00:21:21.710
for writing custom
controller at that point.

00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:24.020
And it was very complex.

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:27.200
So you need to have deep
understanding of low level

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:31.430
Kubernetes APIs to write
something workable.

00:21:31.430 --> 00:21:34.970
And it required a lot
of copying and pasting

00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:37.710
of code, which was
very error prone.

00:21:37.710 --> 00:21:39.870
And the documentation
also was very fragmented

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:41.630
and all over the place.

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:46.350
So we looked at that problem and
we said, how can we solve it?

00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:49.550
How we can make it really
easy to extend Kubernetes?

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:53.540
How we can take all those
extension points and let people

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:56.450
bring their expertise
of writing, managing

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:58.760
the application and
pair it together

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:02.960
with the extension
points and make

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:04.490
it easy to extend Kubernetes.

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:10.000
So let's look at some other
components of Kubebuilder

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:10.500
project.

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:12.960
So the three main
components-- on top you

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:19.080
have this Kubebuilder CLI,
which bootstraps a user project.

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:23.460
And the day to day flow of
either bootstrapping or adding

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:25.620
new APIs and
controllers or updating

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:27.390
the dependencies in
the projects as done

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:29.183
by the Kubebuilder project.

00:22:29.183 --> 00:22:30.600
And then there are
two components,

00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:33.130
controller-tools and
controller-runtime,

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:37.710
which are used by the user
project itself to implement

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:39.990
the APIs and controllers.

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:44.080
So let's look at the tools.

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:46.640
What do they do?

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:48.910
So the first is when
you want to-- let's

00:22:48.910 --> 00:22:51.640
say you decided to write
an API and controller.

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:53.590
You would start with
an empty directory

00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:56.630
and you would like to
bootstrap the project.

00:22:56.630 --> 00:23:00.370
So the first set of commands
which sets up the dependencies,

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:03.867
sets up the right
directory structure

00:23:03.867 --> 00:23:05.950
looking at some of the
things that we have learned

00:23:05.950 --> 00:23:09.360
over the past years by
writing custom controllers,

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.960
so we have baked those
all in these tools.

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:16.060
So what would the make
or Dockerfile look like?

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:19.390
And we'll scaffold the makefile,
which would pretty much have

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:21.700
most common targets
that you would

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:27.070
use in day-to-day development
of your controller.

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:30.020
Then when it comes to
adding APIs and controllers,

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:31.750
scaffolding some of
the boilerplate code

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:33.400
automatically for
you, writing them

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:36.410
up with the right set
of underlying clients

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:39.140
and libraries.

00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:41.030
So this is the part
which Kubebuilder does.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:43.070
And then the
controller tools is--

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:46.130
so like we discussed
about CRDs are

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:48.680
the way to introduce your API.

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:51.380
So it has tools to
generate the CRDs.

00:23:51.380 --> 00:23:54.920
So the tools basically look
at the type start code.

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:57.200
And we'll show you in the
demo how that is done.

00:23:57.200 --> 00:24:00.950
And then generation of the CRDs.

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:03.320
Then the last part, which
is when-- let's say you

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:05.810
have your controller ready.

00:24:05.810 --> 00:24:09.140
You want the manifest
that you need

00:24:09.140 --> 00:24:10.370
for deploying in Kubernetes.

00:24:10.370 --> 00:24:12.370
So you have tools for
generating those, as well.

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:16.880
And then this is the
most important component

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:18.880
of Kubebuilder, which is
the controller-runtime.

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:21.790
Like I discussed before,
the state of the art

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:24.100
was copying and
pasting the code.

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:25.960
So we looked at some
of the common patterns

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:29.230
that you have to do when
you're writing controllers.

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:32.260
For example, you want to
observe certain resources

00:24:32.260 --> 00:24:35.410
in Kubernetes.

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:38.198
Then you want to
invoke reconciliation.

00:24:38.198 --> 00:24:40.240
You want to run this active
reconciliation loops.

00:24:40.240 --> 00:24:43.160
You want to run a number
of threads over there.

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:45.640
And so all those are
some of the things

00:24:45.640 --> 00:24:46.900
that are common patterns.

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:53.620
We abstracted them out and
provided some intuitive APIs

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:56.470
to use that so that
users don't have

00:24:56.470 --> 00:24:58.780
to worry about this low
level-- underneath it

00:24:58.780 --> 00:25:02.380
uses the low level Kubernetes
upstream client libraries,

00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:05.470
but you get the more
intuitive patterns

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:07.060
that you can directly apply.

00:25:07.060 --> 00:25:08.830
Then another is,
very similarly, we

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:10.720
extended it to
admission and conversion

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:12.995
webhooks, which are the APIs.

00:25:12.995 --> 00:25:19.580
And then the third part,
which we have seen,

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:21.770
is about the providing
a dynamic Kubernetes

00:25:21.770 --> 00:25:24.760
client which can work with
any Kubernetes resource.

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:26.950
So that also makes
things really easier

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:29.920
to understand because now you
have a very less API surface

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:33.700
area when it comes to client,
which can talk to any resource.

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:39.860
So essentially, the whole
idea is that we're still

00:25:39.860 --> 00:25:41.750
iterating over these libraries.

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:45.060
And as we're learning more and
more about the best practices,

00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:47.750
we're trying to bake them
either in the code library

00:25:47.750 --> 00:25:51.330
or as in utility functions
that people can use.

00:25:51.330 --> 00:25:53.960
And so another
example is like when

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:56.990
you're running your
controller in the production,

00:25:56.990 --> 00:26:00.890
you want access to all the
metrics to debug any production

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:01.530
scenario.

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:04.550
So the controller-runtime
library automatically

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:07.850
provides other metrics like
the reconciliation errors,

00:26:07.850 --> 00:26:09.350
the number of
reconciliation, what

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.830
is the backlog queue that you
have, and the reconciliations.

00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:18.510
Things like those are
automatically supported.

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:25.580
So I think with that, can
we switch to the terminal?

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:28.770
So we would look at some
of the Kubebuilder flow.

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:30.740
And I will also show
you controller-tools

00:26:30.740 --> 00:26:32.400
and controller-runtime
abstracts.

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:35.325
So let's get started.

00:26:43.478 --> 00:26:43.978
OK.

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:53.550
So right now, we
are in a directory

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:56.570
which we have already
bootstrapped for our project,

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:58.330
the SqlDB operator.

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:04.650
And so let's say you have
downloaded Kubebuilder

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:06.690
release on your laptop.

00:27:06.690 --> 00:27:12.490
And after that what you
will get is Kubebuilder CLI

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:14.180
on your command
prompt, so you can

00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:16.180
basically-- they're not
much commands, actually,

00:27:16.180 --> 00:27:17.160
if you look at.

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:20.960
So the first thing that you want
to do is you want to invoke--

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:23.270
you want to bootstrap
your project.

00:27:23.270 --> 00:27:25.325
So the way to do it would be--

00:27:25.325 --> 00:27:26.700
I've already done
it, so I'm just

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:27.992
going to show the command here.

00:27:27.992 --> 00:27:29.562
So you just run
Kubebuilder in it,

00:27:29.562 --> 00:27:31.020
which will initialize
your project.

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:32.687
It takes a bunch of
parameters, like you

00:27:32.687 --> 00:27:34.902
can specify the license
that you want to include,

00:27:34.902 --> 00:27:36.360
what are the owners
you want to use

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:41.080
in the copy header that shows
up at the top of the file.

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:44.430
Then once you have
that, what you'll see

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:48.980
is it will scaffold the
project structure for you.

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:50.480
So this is pretty
much the structure

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:53.510
that every Kubernetes project
that you'll see follows.

00:27:53.510 --> 00:27:57.140
And so let's look at very
quickly some of the things

00:27:57.140 --> 00:27:58.640
that it did for us.

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:02.000
So it sets up the
go dependencies.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:03.650
You see the Gopkg.lock
and Gopkg.toml

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:05.750
package terminal
file, which is--

00:28:05.750 --> 00:28:07.660
we're using Godep for
managing dependencies.

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:10.670
So we set that up automatically
so that all the Kubernetes

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:12.830
dependencies gets
pulled for you.

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:15.560
So that gets pulled in
the vendor directory here.

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:18.410
Then look at we
automatically create

00:28:18.410 --> 00:28:20.770
Dockerfile, scaffold
the Dockerfile,

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:24.740
which we'll use for building
the controller image that we

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:27.510
can run it in cluster.

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:29.760
Then we scaffold the Makefile.

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:31.260
I can quickly show
you the Makefile.

00:28:31.260 --> 00:28:33.010
So Makefile has some
of the common targets

00:28:33.010 --> 00:28:34.400
that you'll be working.

00:28:34.400 --> 00:28:37.260
So one example would
be you'll be scoping--

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:39.170
let's say you want to run test.

00:28:39.170 --> 00:28:40.820
You want to build
the manager binary.

00:28:40.820 --> 00:28:42.740
You want to run.

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:45.500
You want to install
CRDs in the cluster.

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:47.810
You want to deploy the manifest.

00:28:47.810 --> 00:28:50.870
You want to, let's say,
clean up all the manifest

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:52.160
that you deployed.

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:57.470
So all these common
workflow, it automatically

00:28:57.470 --> 00:29:01.120
is available right off the
bat when you run that command.

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:03.900
Then let's show you a
little bit of the code.

00:29:03.900 --> 00:29:10.300
So we'll scaffold
the main.go, which

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:14.260
is the entry point for your
controller, where it will read

00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:16.450
the config, which
is a Kubernetes

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:19.480
config, whether you're running
on locally or in the cluster.

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:21.430
It will automatically
