00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:55.650
So let's skip this.

00:34:55.650 --> 00:34:59.530
This is where controller
and API code is organized.

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:01.590
So I think now this
would be the right time

00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:03.750
to switch to the next
part, where let's look

00:35:03.750 --> 00:35:06.570
at the SqlDB operator.

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:08.790
So Jun would be
giving us the overview

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:14.890
how the SqlDB operator that we
wrote for this demo is set up.

00:35:14.890 --> 00:35:17.770
So Jun?

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:21.810
JUN TEE: Thanks Sunil for the
explanation about Kubebuilder.

00:35:21.810 --> 00:35:24.000
In this session, we will
show how Kubebuilder

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:26.470
is used to create
a simple operator,

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:30.520
a simple stateful
operator named SqlDB.

00:35:30.520 --> 00:35:33.240
Before diving deep
into the demo,

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:35.490
I wish to provide a
high level overview

00:35:35.490 --> 00:35:38.190
of what the operator does.

00:35:38.190 --> 00:35:44.152
The operator creates and
manages a Postgres cluster.

00:35:44.152 --> 00:35:46.130
It's comprised of two APIs.

00:35:46.130 --> 00:35:51.300
Like Sunil said, it's a SqlDB
API and a SqlBackup API.

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:53.820
The SqlDB API is
used for the creation

00:35:53.820 --> 00:35:57.030
and restore of the
Postgres clusters,

00:35:57.030 --> 00:36:00.690
while the SqlBackup API is
used for backup the data

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:02.760
of the cluster.

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:07.110
Underlying all the Postgres
cluster created by the operator

00:36:07.110 --> 00:36:10.650
we have a shared storage
layer underneath.

00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:14.250
We use a network file system
to start a backup file,

00:36:14.250 --> 00:36:17.010
and then retrieve the backup
file for the restoration

00:36:17.010 --> 00:36:18.120
purpose.

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:21.750
We run the operator
between a GKE cluster

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:26.970
and create the manifest to
create the SqlDB and SqlBackup

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:29.550
resources.

00:36:29.550 --> 00:36:31.420
Our demo workflow
is very simple.

00:36:31.420 --> 00:36:33.790
It's comprised of
only three steps.

00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:37.050
The first step is to create
a SqlDB resource to roll out

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:40.200
a cluster and wait for
the Postgres cluster

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:41.340
to become ready.

00:36:41.340 --> 00:36:43.910
And then we populate
the data to it.

00:36:43.910 --> 00:36:47.700
Please know that for
simplicity reasons, in our demo

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:51.190
we have only stacked one
Postgres database instance.

00:36:51.190 --> 00:36:55.740
So a cluster means only
one instance of a database.

00:36:55.740 --> 00:36:59.340
And in a second session, we
will create a SqlBackup resource

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:02.640
to trigger the backup
of data that we inserted

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:05.280
into the cluster in step 1.

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:08.980
And lastly, we will create
another SqlDB resource

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:10.740
to roll out another cluster.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:14.040
And we will verify that
the data of the old cluster

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:16.205
can be restored to
a second cluster.

00:37:18.850 --> 00:37:20.110
Yeah.

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:24.490
Now let us go over the workflows
for the Postgres database

00:37:24.490 --> 00:37:26.050
instant creation.

00:37:26.050 --> 00:37:31.090
What happens here is that the
users create a SqlDB resource.

00:37:31.090 --> 00:37:34.150
When the controller
observe the creation event,

00:37:34.150 --> 00:37:37.090
the controller creates
a StatefulSet resource.

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:39.910
Within spec of the
StatefulSet resource,

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:44.560
we specify the Postgres
as the container image.

00:37:44.560 --> 00:37:46.810
And then the
StatefulSet controller,

00:37:46.810 --> 00:37:50.800
one of the primitives that
we went over in session one,

00:37:50.800 --> 00:37:53.230
creates the pods.

00:37:53.230 --> 00:37:55.720
And within the pod, there
is the main container

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:58.390
running the Postgres database.

00:37:58.390 --> 00:38:03.340
And we use a Postgres-specific
client, named psql,

00:38:03.340 --> 00:38:05.720
to assess the container.

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:09.490
This is done through a
Kubernetes service resource.

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:12.760
This service is the
[INAUDIBLE] service

00:38:12.760 --> 00:38:16.360
that has post the
container to the client.

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:21.390
And all of this information
is hidden from the end users.

00:38:21.390 --> 00:38:24.400
All the end users have
to do is very simple.

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:28.530
Just by running the kubectl
apply, the manifest file,

00:38:28.530 --> 00:38:32.470
all the users have to do is
to create the SqlDB resource

00:38:32.470 --> 00:38:34.070
and that's it.

00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:37.900
For the Backup flow, what the
users can do with the operator

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:41.840
is that he or she can
create a SqlBackup resource.

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:44.140
The backup resource
has a reference

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:47.110
back to the SqlDB
resource they created

00:38:47.110 --> 00:38:49.000
in the previous workflow.

00:38:49.000 --> 00:38:53.260
And with this information,
the SqlBackup controller

00:38:53.260 --> 00:38:56.830
is able to identify
which container that it

00:38:56.830 --> 00:38:59.320
wants to run a command with.

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:01.950
The controller
will run a command

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:05.490
within the container to
trigger the backup utility

00:39:05.490 --> 00:39:07.220
to perform as the backup.

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:10.030
What happens here is
that a backup file

00:39:10.030 --> 00:39:13.480
will be created by
the utility and stored

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:15.960
into the underlying
network file system.

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:19.070
Please note that for
the network file system,

00:39:19.070 --> 00:39:21.460
we can substitute
it for other storage

00:39:21.460 --> 00:39:27.400
mediums, such as the Google
Cloud storage and the Amazon S3

00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:30.250
and Azure storage.

00:39:30.250 --> 00:39:32.410
It is interchangeable.

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:34.750
And lastly, for the
Restore workflow,

00:39:34.750 --> 00:39:38.290
the users create a
second DB resource.

00:39:38.290 --> 00:39:42.700
But this time, this DB resource
has one additional API field

00:39:42.700 --> 00:39:45.840
that referenced back to
the SqlBackup resource.

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:47.530
Why we want to do this?

00:39:47.530 --> 00:39:49.520
This because we want
to get the backup

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:52.550
file information from
the backup resource.

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:56.170
Using the backup file
name, the SqlDB controller

00:39:56.170 --> 00:39:59.950
is able to fetch the correct
backup file from the underlying

00:39:59.950 --> 00:40:04.840
network file system, and
then call the restore utility

00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:06.670
to perform the restoration.

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:09.580
In the end, a new
cluster will be created

00:40:09.580 --> 00:40:12.790
and the cluster will do
that with a backup data.

00:40:12.790 --> 00:40:17.320
And Sunil will show us all
the workflows through a demo.

