columns that you can configure.

00:42:33.830 --> 00:42:36.640
So since it's a--

00:42:36.640 --> 00:42:39.280
yeah, so this became
ready pretty fast.

00:42:39.280 --> 00:42:40.760
So let's look at--

00:42:40.760 --> 00:42:45.040
we can access this on
this service endpoint.

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:48.580
So status is one where--

00:42:48.580 --> 00:42:50.380
yeah, it is using the
status field here.

00:42:50.380 --> 00:42:54.290
So we're using hard coding
the credentials here.

00:42:54.290 --> 00:42:56.080
So we'll use that.

00:42:56.080 --> 00:42:56.580
OK.

00:42:56.580 --> 00:42:57.450
So we are in.

00:42:57.450 --> 00:42:59.580
So you can see
that what we did is

00:42:59.580 --> 00:43:02.820
we submitted the db1.yaml
using kubectl, not

00:43:02.820 --> 00:43:06.990
using any new sort of tools
to work with our database.

00:43:06.990 --> 00:43:10.230
And then we use the
existing primitives

00:43:10.230 --> 00:43:12.940
like services to expose it.

00:43:12.940 --> 00:43:17.320
And if you look at it as it is
using StatefulSet, so you can--

00:43:17.320 --> 00:43:17.820
not here.

00:43:21.760 --> 00:43:25.730
If you look at it, it
created a db1-statefulset,

00:43:25.730 --> 00:43:27.640
which is actually
running the Postgres pod

00:43:27.640 --> 00:43:29.490
and container inside it.

00:43:29.490 --> 00:43:31.580
So now let's look
at-- we want to take

00:43:31.580 --> 00:43:35.130
a backup, which is what the
Day 2 operation looks like.

00:43:35.130 --> 00:43:40.360
So for backup, what you
want to do is, let's say,

00:43:40.360 --> 00:43:43.630
this is also modeled as
another API, SqlBackup.

00:43:43.630 --> 00:43:45.380
And in this case, it's
a very simple API.

00:43:45.380 --> 00:43:47.340
We're saying we want to
just back up the db1.

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:50.600
And we want to save the
output in this file.

00:43:50.600 --> 00:43:54.130
Now we can just apply
this in a similar way.

00:43:57.340 --> 00:43:59.910
So this is like a Day 2
operation, you can imagine,

00:43:59.910 --> 00:44:01.500
which is taking a backup.

00:44:08.955 --> 00:44:10.170
Oh, OK.

00:44:10.170 --> 00:44:10.670
Sorry.

00:44:16.330 --> 00:44:18.040
So the backup is succeeded.

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:19.990
